./services/platform/backend/MIGRATION.md:136:| knowledge             | done                               | inc 15: search/fetch REUSED verbatim via the ctx shim (org lookup + credential loads + Tier-A retrievable filter re-pointed at 0.5); ingest = 0.5 composition of the exported pieces (extract→embed→indexDocument) on the `rag.index_file` job; default-corpus bootstrap at worker boot. PENDING: transcript RAG (tts), corpus status/repair surfaces, KNOWLEDGE_MIGRATIONS_DIR in the runner image (web corpus crawling landed with websites, inc 67); the conversation lanes it also listed have landed — the CONVERSATION retrievable branch (`decideRetrievable` decides an emailed attachment from the caller's readable conversations, resolved per dispatch from the candidates' own; #3121 step 1), queue-on-bind for emailed attachments (the bind queues `rag.index_file` in its own transaction, #3225), and email-message (`msg:`) refs (#3015, the note at the end of this row) inc 93: the ADMIN CONFIG surface for the data-residency page — `domains/knowledge/admin.ts` (the 0.4 `knowledge/{actions,file_actions}` re-orchestrated; paths/schemas/history layout byte-identical via the reused `connection.ts` resolvers + file_io; the four-line serializers twinned): connection read/write (password sidecar: set/replace, empty-string REMOVES, absent keeps; org pool URL busted on change)/delete/probe (stored-password fallback + pgvector/ParadeDB hints), embedding read/write (SSRF-gated baseUrl; history snapshots)/delete, and `GET /embedding/recommendations` (direct-credential providers × shipped catalogs through the reused `pickEmbeddingRecommendations`). Routes on `/api/app/knowledge/*` behind the orgSettings write gate. Frontend: 3 ACTION_QUERY reads + recommendations + 5 writes. Probes: save→view hasPassword→LIVE pg probe ok (the itest DB itself)→embedding save/read→recommendations 200→both deletes revert to unconfigured. BROWSER: embedding form save → 'Configured' badge + `knowledge/embedding.json` on disk. inc 114 CLOSES the row. KNOWLEDGE_MIGRATIONS_DIR in the runner image was a REAL production bug and is fixed: `findMigrationsDir` falls back to walking up the module path for a repo checkout, which a container does not have, so preparing a new corpus (the deployment default on first boot, and every BYO per-org database) degraded to the "apply them yourself" remedy. The platform image now COPIES `services/db/migrations/knowledge-db` — into the builder stage (so the dev image inherits it) and into the runner — and names it in `KNOWLEDGE_MIGRATIONS_DIR` in both, with a guard test that fails if either copy or either env line disappears. The rest of the row's PENDING list names work that does not exist to port: TRANSCRIPT RAG is 0.4's OWN documented deferral (`transcribe_audio.ts`: "a deliberate follow-up … `transcriptRagStatus` stays unset") and 0.5 mirrors it exactly, including the captions branch that DOES index; the CONVERSATION/EMAIL retrievable branch is wired (`domains/conversations/search-chat.ts` behind the chat shim's `searchConversationsForChat`); and CORPUS STATUS/REPAIR is `corpus_status.getStatuses`, ported and consumed by the inc-104 RAG watchdog — no repair surface exists in either version. #3015: inbound email BODIES index under message refs (`lib/knowledge/message-ref.ts`, `msg:<message id>`). `addMessageToConversation` queues `rag.index_message` in the insert's own transaction for an inbound `email` message a connector delivered (`connector_name`, so a message a member logs by hand never indexes) with a non-empty body, on a conversation not marked spam; `domains/knowledge/message-index.ts` reads an HTML body down to text (`lib/knowledge/html-to-text.ts`), heads its chunks with the subject and sender, stamps the conversation id, runs the secret scan and the PII policy over the header as well as the body (`prepareDocument` masks or refuses the header and the stored name like the text, for every lane), and ends quietly on the refusals no retry changes (no embedding model, a refused credential or width). The retrievable filter's `msg:` branch decides by the message's conversation (`conversationAssignmentAllows`, a live lifecycle, not spam), and only for a door that asks (`includeConversationMessages`: the chat tools, which cite a body hit as a `conversation` row and `rag_fetch` its whole text wrapped as mail; the SQL pre-filter excludes message rows for every other door, the sandbox bridge and the MCP door included, and searches them as a partition with its own candidate pool, so bodies a member may not read never push a document out of the page). Outbound mail and API-mirrored conversations are not indexed. Mail stored before this change is not backfilled; a mailbox connected after it indexes every inbound email its first sync imports, bounded by the queue's batch and the organization's embedding limiter. Release: liveness is the inbound message row of a conversation not marked spam (`assessMessageRefLiveness`); `deleteConversation` and the retention window queue `knowledge.release_refs` in the transaction that deletes the conversations, a spam verdict queues it and lifting the verdict queues the index again (`domains/conversations/message-corpus.ts`), and the corpus reconcile walks message refs apart from blob refs, from a random pivot. Real-Postgres proof: the `checkInboundEmailBodies` lane (`domains/knowledge/message-index.integration.ts`). Emailed ATTACHMENTS are mail too: 0.4's `indexFileBlob` stamped an attachment's corpus row with its conversation and the 0.5 port dropped it, so the pre-filter read the row as an org-wide hub row and the chat labelled and served it as a plain document. `indexUploadedFile` stamps it again (`emailedAttachmentConversation`: an unbound file row's conversation, unless an active document holds its ref, which it then indexes as, under that document's scope — the ref's holder, the lowest-id active document when several share the ref as WebDAV COPY twins and two documents bound from one upload do, which a file filed into another twin indexes as too and whose scope every scope writer writes), the nightly corpus reconcile backfills rows indexed before (`reconcileMailAttachmentStamps`, which walks every attachment each night and also releases the corpus copy of one whose conversation was deleted or marked spam before its lane queued a release; a second walk over the stamped corpus rows takes the stamp off a row no attachment backs any more — filed into or held by a document, or a thread/chat file — reading the backing again after the clear so a ref that turned back into an attachment meanwhile gets its stamp back (one of a deleted or spam conversation before its corpus release), or releases it when nothing keeps it in the corpus, its bytes and a trashed file row that only remembers them included when nothing references them, and a byte delete that fails there or in the reconcile's blob walk re-queues `knowledge.release_refs`, in jobs of at most 500 refs as the mail lanes and a task retire queue theirs (`queueRefRelease`), its log line saying whether it was re-queued; each clear locks at most 100 corpus rows in a transaction of its own, its recheck read under a statement timeout on an app connection taken before the lock and under a 15 s bound of its own for a connection that stops answering during the clear, which then rolls back and the pass goes on without that connection, and one that cannot finish rolls back for the next night; the log keeps the two walks' counts apart, and the recheck's restamps and releases apart from the clears; the scope pass leaves conversation stamps alone, so its drift count stays scope writes that failed or never ran — a per-edit sync the corpus refused, an edit whose sync writes less than the whole scope (a WebDAV folder MOVE into a team folder re-stamps the paths alone), or a change of a shared ref's holder on a lane that does not re-stamp it — and it and the per-edit syncs write a shared ref from its holder alone, so an edit of either twin is not drift the next night; a write that moves a shared ref's holder without editing a scope — a WebDAV COPY, trash (a folder cascade and an overwritten destination included) or PUT overwrite, a governance Trash restore, any hard delete (`purgeDocument`), a sync update's or an entry delete's old ref — re-stamps the ref from its new holder after its commit (`syncRagRefHolderScopes`); the first night after the release that makes every scope writer write the holder (#3808) the pass corrects, once, every row an older release stamped from a copy of the ref rather than its holder, a burst of drift that is no failed sync), and the retrievable filter decides an attachment exactly like a body (`decideMailRetrievable`, which also refuses a scopeless caller); bodies and attachments are one `mail` partition that only the chat tools ask for, the corpus pre-filter keeps every conversation-stamped row out of every other door, the chat wraps an attachment as untrusted mail (provenance from the file rows while a row is unstamped), mail rows never take part in content-hash clones, and corpus-liveness reads an attachment of a deleted or spam conversation as dead while its bytes stay — so delete and spam release its corpus copy, lifting spam re-queues `rag.index_file`, and the retention window releases it with the conversation. Real-Postgres proof: the `checkEmailedAttachments` lane (`domains/knowledge/attachment-mail.integration.ts`). |
./services/platform/backend/MIGRATION.md:168:| websites              | done                               | inc 67: migration 0045 `app.websites` (the org-facing registration/status/failure-ledger row; UNIQUE (org, domain)) + `domains/websites/{service,routes}.ts` + `rest/v1-websites.ts`. The corpus side stays in the KNOWLEDGE database untouched: the whole data seam (`knowledge/crawl.ts` — register/deregister/membership-guarded reads/BM25-with-ILIKE-fallback search) and the crawl ENGINE (`knowledge/crawl_action.ts`: discovery via robots/sitemaps/BFS, polite budgeted fetch with content-type dispatch, boilerplate paragraph-hash stripping, chunk + embed (NULL vectors when no embedding model — keyword-only until a re-scan), 404 prune, continuation chain with corpus-claim takeover) are REUSED VERBATIM — the two handler bodies hoisted (`scanWebsiteImpl`/`scanDueWebsitesImpl`, 0.4 wrappers delegate; suites 262+231 green) and run on a ctx shim: knowledge shim (org lookups + embedder), the sandbox session verbs behind the RENDER lane (`renderUrlsInSandbox` imported as-is; quota errors stay infra failures), this service's recordScanFailure/clearScanFailures/listWebsitesForScanScheduling handlers, and a scheduler seam mapping the self-chain onto `websites.scan` (retryLimit 0 — the engine records its own failures; the 5-min `websites.scan_due` schedule is the retry) and the row-sync fan-out onto singleton-keyed `websites.row_sync` jobs. Registration (`websites.register` job) = corpus upsert (site or curated URL LIST — list entries validated on-domain, re-post merges, kind only widens) + first-scan kick + best-effort homepage title/description + delayed row sync. The failure LEDGER ported exactly (TALE-PROJECT-106: failures recorded on the row because the corpus that failed is unreachable): attempts advance the scheduler clock (bounded retry window), 3 connection-class failures pause + notify org admins ONCE (`websiteScanPaused` bell), resume clears + re-kicks. Routes `/api/app/websites` (list keyset + filters + palette search, count, create, patch (interval syncs corpus cadence — the 0.4 CRAWLER_WEBSITE_NOT_FOUND branch is vestigial: the op silently no-ops for non-members), delete (best-effort deregister; last member takes the corpus domain), resume, sync-statuses (hourly debounce, transient states exempt), pages (with the 0.4 fetch-pages debounce via metadata.lastStatusSyncAt), chunks, search); REST `/api/v1/websites` family route-for-route with `rest_api.ts` (list/create incl. URL lists/get/patch/delete/pages/per-site sync answering 'syncing' fire-and-forget/search). Integration (fake site via global-fetch interception — safeFetch string-guards pass a public-looking domain): register→scan (3 pages, chunks, corpus completed, row synced active 3/3, pages/chunks/search reads), drift (hash flip re-indexes in place, 404 prunes page+chunks), the failure ledger end to end (pause bell exactly once, isDueForScan false while paused, resume clears), REST family + URL-list lane + delete deregistering the corpus. NOT in the harness: the sandboxed RENDER of html pages (needs the browser image — the lane is wired; text/document pages cover the in-process path)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       